w-post-body-paragraph oi oj jf ok b ol qd on oo op qe or os ot qf ov ow ox =
qg oz pa pb qh pd pe pf iz cv" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Broken Authen=
tication occurs when an API=E2=80=99s authentication mechanisms can be bypa=
ssed or abused, often due to weak password policies or the absence of prote=
ctions like rate-limiting. In this scenario, a customer logs in and discove=
rs they can update their account details =E2=80=94 including their password=
 =E2=80=94 using a PATCH endpoint that enforces only minimal password requi=
rements. This weak policy suggests other users might also be using insecure=
 passwords. By identifying the API=E2=80=99s failure message (=E2=80=9CInva=
lid Credentials=E2=80=9D), collecting high-value target emails, and using f=
fuf to fuzz both email and password parameters simultaneously, an attacker =
successfully brute-forces a valid password for a privileged customer accoun=
t. Without rate-limiting, the authentication endpoint allows thousands of a=
ttempts per second, making account takeover trivial.</p><p id=3D"f177" clas=
s=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph oi oj jf ok b ol om on oo op oq or os ot ou ov =
ow ox oy oz pa pb pc pd pe pf iz cv" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">To prev=
ent Broken Authentication, login endpoints must enforce strict rate-limitin=
g, blocking repeated failed attempts from the same user or IP. APIs should =
also implement strong password policies requiring long, complex, non-guessa=
ble passwords and disallowing commonly used credentials. Additional protect=
ions =E2=80=94 such as password history enforcement, expiration policies, a=
nd multi-factor authentication (MFA) =E2=80=94 further reduce the risk of b=
rute-force attacks. When passwords are strong and brute-forcing becomes imp=
ractical, attackers may shift to brute-forcing OTPs or security question an=
swers, so these mechanisms must also be designed with high entropy and rate=
-limit protections.</p></div></div><div class=3D"qw"><div class=3D"am hd"><=
