ph=3D"">Broken Function Level Authorization (BFLA) occurs when an API allow=
s users to invoke privileged endpoints despite lacking the required permiss=
ions. Unlike BOLA, where the user can access an endpoint but views data the=
y shouldn=E2=80=99t, BFLA happens when the user is not authorized to access=
 the function at all. In this scenario, a customer with no assigned roles s=
igns in and discovers the /api/v1/products/discounts endpoint, which should=
 only be accessible to users with the ProductDiscounts_GetAll role. Despite=
 this restriction, the endpoint exposes all product discount data, revealin=
g sensitive business information.</p><p id=3D"4007" class=3D"pw-post-body-p=
aragraph oi oj jf ok b ol om on oo op oq or os ot ou ov ow ox oy oz pa pb p=
c pd pe pf iz cv" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This vulnerability arises =
because the backend fails to enforce role-based access control at the funct=
ion level. To prevent BFLA, the /api/v1/products/discounts endpoint must va=
lidate the user=E2=80=99s roles in the source code before executing the ope=
ration. Only users explicitly granted ProductDiscounts_GetAll should be abl=
e to retrieve discount data, ensuring unauthorized actors cannot access pri=
vileged functionality or sensitive information.</p></div></div><div class=
